Understand clients in dynamic.xyz(uses viem under the hood)
=============================================================
Dynamic.xyz or its packages like "@dynamic-labs/ethereum" or "@dynamic-labs/sdk-react-core" uses viem under the hood.
And it exposes those clients to interact with blockchain either for doing public actions or wallet actions.


import { useDynamicContext } from "@dynamic-labs/sdk-react-core";
import {isEthereumWallet} from "@dynamic-labs/ethereum";


 const { primaryWallet } = useDynamicContext(); // It has only generic blockchain methods

 if (isEthereumWallet(primaryWallet)) {
      const walletClient = await primaryWallet.getWalletClient();
      const publicClient = await primaryWallet.getPublicClient();
      // ✅ Now TypeScript knows this wallet has that method
    }


Note:
- This method :
	if (isEthereumWallet(primaryWallet)){
	} 
enables all blockchain methods for either ethereum or all EVM supported chains as we are checking for @dynamic-labs/ethereum" EVM chains.

- Same things we can do for other chains by using these packages: "@dynamic-labs/solana" or "@dynamic-labs/sui" by using different set of methods.

•	The blockchain-specific methods (getWalletClient, etc.) live in chain-specific packages (@dynamic-labs/ethereum, @dynamic-labs/solana, etc.).


We are talking about ethereum or EVM supported chains(like rayls) currently.
Please remember for talking with Ethereum/EVM supported chains we can use "viem", but for solana or sui, we will not use it, we will use something different.


Dynamic’s goal is to abstract away wallet-specific quirks (MetaMask, Coinbase Wallet, WalletConnect, etc.) and give you a consistent interface.

So instead of manually connecting to an injected provider (window.ethereum), you:
• Call getWalletClient() to talk to the wallet via a standardized, Dynamic-managed interface.
• Call getPublicClient() for general blockchain reads (even before a wallet is connected).

But for dynamic, we need wallet to be connected then only we can interact with blockchains:

Look at this:

const walletClient = await primaryWallet.getWalletClient();
const publicClient = await primaryWallet.getPublicClient();


Why There Are Two Clients, Not One ?

The separation is intentional and rooted in Viem’s design:
	•	You can query public blockchain data without needing a wallet connection.
	•	You only use the wallet (and trigger user interactions) when writing or signing.

This design encourages safe and efficient DApp architecture:
	•	Your app loads and displays data quickly via PublicClient.
	•	Only user-triggered actions (e.g. “Mint”, “Stake”, “Add Token”) use WalletClient.


