"Tokens never live inside the wallet; they always live inside the token smart contract on the blockchain.""

In the above context, then why do we have to import tokens in metamask wallet before using them ?
Importing does not mean living inside the wallet, then what does it mean  ?
---------------------------

✅ Why Do We Import Tokens in MetaMask if Tokens Don’t Live in the Wallet?

*Because “importing a token” only tells MetaMask:

➡️ “Please display this token balance for my address.”
It does NOT tell:
✖️ “Store this token inside the wallet.”
✖️ “Move the token into the wallet.”


✅ What “Import Token” Actually Means
✔️ 1. MetaMask does NOT automatically know every token in existence

Ethereum has:
- thousands of networks
- millions of tokens

MetaMask cannot automatically detect them all.

So when you “import a token,” you are simply giving MetaMask the token contract address, so it can:

- read your balance from that contract
- show the balance in the UI
- show you transfers related to that token
- let you perform UI-based actions like send/swap

It is only a display/visibility feature.

Nothing happens on-chain when you import a token.


✅ 2. Importing does NOT bring the token into your wallet

Your tokens were already there (on the blockchain) under your address.
Or by default all the tokens (millions) are on the blockchain and by default their balacnce for your address is zero.
Like for this wallet , i have "RXP" token as 0 but "RXP" token exists on blockchain.

Importing simply tell metamask or blockchain that you are interested to make deposit/swap etc transaction only on that specific token.

MetaMask just wasn’t showing them because it didn’t know which token contract to query but importing makes it aware.


✅ 3. Why MetaMask requires importing before interacting

On any blockchain network there are millions of tokens, so we need to specify which token we are interested.
And thats why we import it to give metamask information about that token.
Also, we inform metamask, which contract to call when sending/approving tokens.


✅ Does MetaMask “call” the smart contract or does the SDK/library call it?

MetaMask is not a smart-contract caller. The SDK/library calls the smart contract — MetaMask only signs the transaction.
And sends signed transactions to the blockchain.


It is ethers.js / web3.js / wagmi / viem (the SDK) that:
	- builds the transaction request
	- And sends this request to metamask to sign it, once signed it is broadcasted on the blockchain.
	The specific contract address which you specified in the sdk code will execute this transaction.




Presence of tokens on a network
===============================

Our testnet did not have USDC but our devnet has USDC. I wanted to test some token related things on testnet. what does it mean >?
How will we know what tokes are theri on our own custom raysls L1 testnet as a blockchain dev ?
Do we simply need to ask our lead to confirm resence of any token on testnet or so ?


✅ 1. Tokens do NOT automatically exist on every network

A token (USDC, USDT, RXP, etc.) only exists on a network if its token smart contract is deployed on that network.

Meaning:

If the USDC contract is NOT deployed on your testnet → USDC does not exist on that testnet.

If the USDC contract is deployed on your devnet → USDC exists on the devnet.

Tokens don’t magically exist on all networks — someone must deploy the ERC-20 token contract there.



✅ 2. What “your testnet does not have USDC” means

It simply means:

“The USDC token contract has NOT been deployed on your testnet.”

So your testnet blockchain has no record, no contract, no balances, no metadata for USDC.

If you call a USDC token address on that testnet → it won’t work
(because that contract is not deployed on that chain).


✅ 3. How do we know what tokens exist on our custom RAYLS L1 testnet?

As a blockchain developer, you have three ways to know:

Method 1 — Check your block explorer (if available)
--------
If your chain has an explorer (like Etherscan), you would see a list of deployed contracts, including tokens.

But many custom testnets do NOT have explorers.


Method 2 — Check deployment logs / registry
--------
If your testnet is custom, someone on your team must have deployed the tokens manually.


Method 3 — Ask the lead / team member responsible
--------
- This is usually what happens in real teams.

Your lead/devops/blockchain engineer decides:

- What tokens exist on testnet

- What contracts get deployed

- Whether USDC, USDT, new tokens, etc., should exist


If the team says:

“Testnet does not have USDC.”

It simply means:

“We didn’t deploy the USDC token contract on testnet.”


🧠 Important concept: Tokens do NOT come with the chain

A blockchain (mainnet, testnet, devnet) does NOT come with any tokens by default.

Every token must be manually deployed as a smart contract.

Ethereum doesn’t ship with USDC.
Polygon doesn’t ship with USDC.
Your L1 testnet doesn’t ship with USDC.

Circle (the company behind USDC) deploys the USDC contract on each network where they want USDC to exist.

If they haven’t deployed it → USDC does not exist.
















